speechtext:
No, and I do not mean to, but I should be delighted if I were to have the privilege of hearing my right hon. friend expound the Liberal theory when he did not feel that his words would be taken down verbatim. No, the fact is clear and cannot be dodged; we are not going to improve our condition in Canada in any steady way except by concentration upon our problems within Canada. I think the minister will agree with that. And I propose to devote the next part of this speech to showing how internal conditions in Canada have been neglected by the present administration.
Let us go back to July 24, 1933. The present leader of the government was then the leader of the opposition. He was speaking in Virden, Manitoba, on the date I have mentioned. In part he said:
And what would the Liberal party do if returned to power?
It would deal with unemployment through a great national commission, including the provinces and the dominion, utilizing those great
bodies such as the Red Cross. This great commission would mould the relief agencies into one great cooperative commonwealth in the real sense of the word. And it would thus have solved the unemployment problem.
Apparently the right hon. gentleman was successful in persuading the people of Canada that such a commission would find a solution. At least he so convinced his new Minister of Labour, who ably caught the torch flung from Virden, Manitoba. At page 542 of Hansard of February 26, 1936, we read these words by the minister:
It is expected that through the instrumentality of the proposed national employment commission, worlcing in cooperation with business and industrial undertakings, additional avenues of employment will be found. A greater degree of cooperation will be sought in the national interest between the government and those agencies which have the power and understanding to meet the problem.
I wonder whether the people of Canada or the minister himself would have found the idea of this commission so palatable if they had realized prior to the election that after the much-heralded commission had deliberated at a cost of $325,549.26 and at long last produced its report, that report would have become essentially and automatically a dead letter and would be filed by one of the supporters of the government on the floor of the house beside his seat; and that as a net result of the efforts of the national employment commission the problem of unemployment would have been so little changed.
I have in my hand the final report of the national employment commission, dated January 26, 1938. I have not the slightest hesitation in saying, what I said in reviewing the report in this house last year, that in most ways it is a most praiseworthy report, the sort of report one would expect to be brought in by a commission presided over by so able a Canadian as Arthur B. Purvis and supported by so many other able Canadians. If time permitted I could go through this report and point out recommendation after recommendation which has been entirely ignored by the government. For example, turning to page 35 of the report we read the following:
Works undertaken in such programs should be those which have a definite value in promoting the competitive strength of Canadian industry, and in improving the conditions of life in the dominion. They may properly include:-
Building of highways to permit the development of mining, tourist and other resources;
Development and preservation of tourist regions;
Projects for the improvement and protection of public health and safety (such as provision of municipal pure water supply and sewage disposal projects, elimination of railway and highway grade crossings, et cetera);
Slum clearance and low rental housing schemes in urban areas;

Unemployment and Agricultural Distress
Forestry plans for extending and preserving forests;
Reclamation and conservation projects;
Land clearance and settlement projects where the conditions are clearly favourable to successful settlement.
Then turning to pages 20-21-22 of the intermediate report we find listed a summary of commission recommendations up to and including the time of the interim report. I could deal with all these recommendations, but I shall not tire the house by taking the time to do so. I will deal with four of them only; the second, for the national administration of the employment service of Canada; the fourth, covering the suggestions for the care and provision for homeless adults; the fifth, in regard to housing, and the sixth, in regard to the training and reconditioning of youth.
Take first the second recommendation, in regard to the employment service of Canada. The recommendation itself suggests that the service should be surveyed with the thought in mind of changing the location of the offices and rearranging the personnel of the office staff and the salaries paid, and that the whole organization of the national employment service should be put under federal administration. Such a complete survey was made and a report was submitted; but was there any change in this vital matter? There was none. Why? The minister gave the house the reason the other day in reply to the hon. member for Comox-Alberni (Mr. Neill), on the introduction of his unemployment insurance resolution. At that time the minister said in essence it was the plan of the government that if unemployment insurance had become a "possibility" the act would have been administered through the employment bureau, but that since unemployment insurance had become essentially a dead letter, the employment bureau had remained unchanged. Such a skinny excuse! There is a further reason, perhaps, which the minister might have offered, namely, that the, change would have cost some $500,000, I believe, and that the government could not afford this expense, or did not deem it advisable to undertake it. Well, Mr. Speaker, each year hundreds of thousands of applications for work are made through this bureau, and the minister knows not only as a result of the report of the commission but also from his own experience that there is a high degree of inefficiency and ineffectiveness as a result of the fact that these offices are not under federal administration.
